Lebanon is reopening after a two months  lockdown a major outbreak of the corona  virus appears to have been averted  but the pandemic devastated an already  collapsing economy many businesses  didn't survive it has been dead before  some of our projects we had to take the  right decisions if we need to go on we  closed Hamra on May 1st it was saddest  day in my whole life maybe  and for us we closed a wonderful place a  wonderful project that couldn't sustain  financially at all anymore many  restaurants won't be reopening with the  recent collapse of the local currency  the Libra it's become more expensive to  operate them but this sector was hit  hard months before because of Lebanon's  worst financial crisis in decades nearly  1,000 outlets have closed since  September  150,000 japanese employees are employed  in this sector translates into 150  thousand families who live from this  sector 25,000 were laid off between  October and January we don't know the  numbers now but I can tell you 120,000  employees are sitting at home today in  another challenge restaurants that are  back in business are obliged to operate  at 30% capacity to protect against the  coronavirus rising poverty and inflation  also means fewer customers restaurant  owners are asking for a relief from  taxes and loans to be reduced hotels are  also asking for assistance but the state  is close to bankruptcy the hospitality  sector used to be a pillar of the  economy contributing 10% of the GDP it's  also the biggest employer after the  public sector at least 18,000 hotel  employees have lost their jobs in recent  months losses have been accumulating for  years blamed partly on land being closed  off due to the war in neighboring Syria  as well as political instability that  has kept tourists away now the pandemic  has brought this sector to our halt  we're closing simply because we lost the  hope of you know a better tomorrow or to  continue  for 70 years the Bristol Hotel hosted  international conferences and  dignitaries never closing even during  the 15-year civil war that ended in 1990  this landmark is now a symbol of what  many describe as one of Lebanon's  darkest times senator al Jazeera Beirut
 

